  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128]}^{\u2032}}{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-5)-48}{2x-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[2{x}^{3}-10x-24]}^{\u2032}}{{[x-3]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-10}{1} \\\\  & =6(3)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
